module

public import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.FiniteType
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.TensorProduct.Basic
public import WeilConjectures.Mathlib.Topology.KrullDimension

/-!
# Dimension at a point under extension of the ground field

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Lemma 10.116.6, Tag 00P4
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-dimension-at-point-field-extension).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v w

open TensorProduct

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- The dimension at a point of the spectrum of a finite type algebra is preserved by any
extension of its ground field. The map `algebraMap k K` is the given field homomorphism;
`S ⊗[k] K` has its right-factor `K`-algebra structure. The displayed prime-spectrum map
contracts the prime along `s ↦ s ⊗ₜ 1`.

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Lemma 10.116.6, Tag 00P4
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-dimension-at-point-field-extension).

Proof:
1. Choose a surjection `P = k[x₁, …, xₙ] → S` with finitely generated kernel `I`, using
   def-weil-pbc-finite-type-algebra, def-weil-pbc-mv-polynomial-ring,
   lem-weil-pbc-polynomial-ring-field-regular-ring and def-weil-pbc-noetherian-ring.
   By lem-weil-pbc-presentation-base-change, scalar extension gives a surjection
   `P_K = K[x₁, …, xₙ] → S ⊗[k] K` with kernel generated by the images of generators of `I`.
   The coefficient-extension map `P → P_K` and the inclusion `S → S ⊗[k] K` commute with
   these surjections. The inverse images `q'` and `q'_K` of the two primes therefore also
   correspond by contraction.
2. The polynomial rings are Noetherian by lem-weil-pbc-polynomial-ring-field-regular-ring;
   their quotients are Noetherian by lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-algebra-noetherian.
   The field homomorphism gives def-pc-field-extension. Both scalar-extension maps are
   faithfully flat by lem-weil-pbc-field-extension-base-change-faithfully-flat, hence flat
   by def-weil-pbc-faithfully-flat-ring-map and def-weil-pbc-flat-ring-map. For the polynomial
   map use the identification `P ⊗[k] K ≃ P_K` from lem-weil-pbc-presentation-base-change.
3. The surjection `P_K → S ⊗[k] K` identifies `P_K / q'P_K` with
   `(S ⊗[k] K) / q(S ⊗[k] K)`: it maps the extended ideal onto the extended ideal, and its
   kernel lies in the first ideal. The primes induced by `q'_K` and `q_K` correspond, so
   their heights in these two quotient rings agree; call this common height `h`.
4. The heights are finite by
   lem-weil-pbc-polynomial-quotient-dimension-at-point-height-difference.
   Apply lem-weil-pbc-flat-height-formula to both vertical maps: each upper prime height
   is its lower prime height plus `h`. Subtraction gives
   `height(q') - height(q) = height(q'_K) - height(q_K)`.
5. The presentation isomorphisms induce homeomorphisms of spectra (def-weil-affine-spectrum),
   which preserve the minimum of the dimensions of open neighbourhoods
   (def-kun-topological-dimension-at-point). By
   lem-weil-pbc-polynomial-quotient-dimension-at-point-height-difference, the two dimensions
   are respectively `n - (height(q') - height(q))` and
   `n - (height(q'_K) - height(q_K))`. Step 4 identifies them. -/
theorem topologicalKrullDimAt_spec_tensorProduct_field_extension
    {k : Type u} {K : Type v} {S : Type w} [Field k] [Field K] [CommRing S]
    [Algebra k K] [Algebra k S] [Algebra.FiniteType k S]
    (qK : Spec (.of (S ⊗[k] K))) :
    topologicalKrullDimAt (X := Spec (.of S))
        (PrimeSpectrum.comap
          (Algebra.TensorProduct.includeLeftRingHom (R := k) (A := S) (B := K)) qK) =
      topologicalKrullDimAt qK := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
